  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 PM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nA cold front located near the Mississippi River this afternoon will \ncontinue to shift eastward overnight as an upper level trough over \nthe region progresses eastward, ingesting an upper level low and \nremnant moisture from former tropical system Polo. 12Z soundings in \nthe southern Plains reflect over 2 inches of precipitable water, \nwhich lends credence to localized accumulations of rainfall of \n2 to 4 inches shown in a few high-res models by Friday morning. \nAmounts of this range would require nearly stationary corridors \nof multiple rounds of showers or thunderstorms (training). \nNevertheless, most areas can expect one half to one inch \namounts. A few thunderstorms will also be possible through \ntonight given low values of CAPE forecast, although recently \nhave not detected any lightning from showers around the IL River\nand westward.\n\nPrecipitation should come to an end by sunrise from I-55 \nnorthwestward, but could linger past noon from I-70 southward. \nNevertheless, dry conditions will be in place across the area by \nFriday night as high pressure builds into the region from the \nnorthwest. High pressure will remain dominant through next week, \nalthough a few dry cold fronts could modulate temperatures.\nHighs should reach only mid 60s to around 70 Friday and Saturday, \nwarm a bit for Sunday with lower to mid 70s, then cool back to \naround 70 for Monday and Tuesday following a front Sunday. \nTemperatures warm a bit Wednesday and Thursday to mid and upper 70s. \nAfter lows still fairly mild in much of the area tonight, with upper \n50s to mid 60s east of I-55 and lower to mid 50s to the west, lows \nshould generally be seasonably cool with 40s to around 50 the next \nseveral days.",
